  "worked_solution": "To determine the polar coordinates of the intersection points, we will solve the following system of equations for $r$ and $\\theta :$  \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}r=3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe begin by setting the equations equal to each other and solve for $\\theta .$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation in the interval $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}),$ we obtain ${\\theta }_{1}≈{191.5}^{∘}$ and ${\\theta }_{2}≈348.5,$ rounded to $1$ decimal place. \n\nNow, we use the equation $r=4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ to find the $r$-coordinates of the intersection points: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1} & =4+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{1}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4 \\\\ {r}_{2} & =4+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{2}=4+3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5})=\\frac{17}{5}=3.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, polar coordinates of the points of intersection are\n\n$\\,$ $(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{191.5}^{∘})\\,$ and $\\,(r,\\theta )≈(3.4,{348.5}^{∘}).$",
